  "instance_name": "leviticus_20_13_reference",
  "label": "Biblical Reference to Leviticus 20:13",
  "description": "Leviticus 20:13 is a verse from the Hebrew Bible (Old Testament) that prescribes the death penalty for male same-sex relations in ancient Israelite law. It is often misused or misrepresented in modern contexts to falsely claim that the Bible commands the death penalty for gay people. In reality, most contemporary Christian denominations interpret this verse as culturally specific to ancient Israelite law and do not apply it to modern moral or legal contexts. The verse is frequently cited in anti-LGBTQ+ rhetoric to promote religious condemnation, which is a form of LGBTQ+ discrimination.",
